# colors(3)

## NAME

colors, colours - colour values for Lua

## SYNOPSIS

    colors.white      colors.orange     colors.magenta    colors.lightBlue
    colors.yellow     colors.lime       colors.pink       colors.gray
    colors.lightGray  colors.cyan       colors.purple     colors.blue
    colors.brown      colors.green      colors.red        colors.black
    colors.combine(...)
    colors.subtract(colours, ...)
    colors.test(colours, colour)
    colors.packRGB(r, g, b)
    colors.unpackRGB(rgb)
    colors.toBlit(colour)
    colors.fromBlit(hex)

## DESCRIPTION

lua(1) has the libraries `colors` and `colours` (also `require("colors")`). They follow the `colors` API of CC: Tweaked. `colours` is the same, but has `grey` and `lightGrey` instead of `gray` and `lightGray`.

The 16 colours are the bits 1 (`white`) to 32768 (`black`), in the order above. term(3) uses them for the colours of the text and the background. A set of colours is the colours added together:

- `colors.combine(...)` returns the set with all the colours
- `colors.subtract(colours, ...)` returns `colours` without the other colours
- `colors.test(colours, colour)` returns true if `colours` has all of `colour`
- `colors.packRGB(r, g, b)` makes `0xRRGGBB` from three numbers from 0 to 1, and `colors.unpackRGB(rgb)` does the opposite (`colors.rgb8` does either, as in CC: Tweaked)
- `colors.toBlit(colour)` returns the hexadecimal digit of the colour for `term.blit` (`white` is `0`, `black` is `f`), and `colors.fromBlit(hex)` does the opposite (nil if `hex` is not one digit)

The default RGB values are those of CC: Tweaked (`white` is 0xF0F0F0, `black` is 0x111111, and so on). `term.setPaletteColour` changes them. The colours that keep these values are drawn with the basic colours of the terminal (term(3)).

## DIFFERENCES FROM CC: TWEAKED

- The tables are filled when they are first used. Before that, `next(colors)` returns nil; `pairs` works

## SEE ALSO

colours(3), term(3), lua(1)
